\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Leave-one-pattern-out on the pooled baseline (full set: 79.8\%). One pattern, \texttt  {\textbackslash bincorrect\textbackslash b}, carries 10.7\,pp; without it the baseline falls to 69.1\%, below the five-feature pipeline's 74.3\%. We therefore report the baseline as a bounded 69--80\% rather than a single figure.}}{13}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:patterns}{{8}{13}{Leave-one-pattern-out on the pooled baseline (full set: 79.8\%). One pattern, \texttt {\textbackslash bincorrect\textbackslash b}, carries 10.7\,pp; without it the baseline falls to 69.1\%, below the five-feature pipeline's 74.3\%. We therefore report the baseline as a bounded 69--80\% rather than a single figure}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Per-variant accuracy (\%) across 12 deception paraphrases. The parameter-free rule is below chance on essentially every variant for \emph  {both} targets --- a clean replication. The probe-based LR is not: it is near-invariant on Mistral~7B (IIS $0.938$) but markedly wording-sensitive on Qwen~2.5~14B (IIS $0.367$), driven largely by the adversarial phrasing D7.}}{14}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:iis}{{9}{14}{Per-variant accuracy (\%) across 12 deception paraphrases. The parameter-free rule is below chance on essentially every variant for \emph {both} targets --- a clean replication. The probe-based LR is not: it is near-invariant on Mistral~7B (IIS $0.938$) but markedly wording-sensitive on Qwen~2.5~14B (IIS $0.367$), driven largely by the adversarial phrasing D7}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Threshold sweep for the RC$\,\geq \!k$ rule. $k\!=\!1$ is the best fixed default but is not uniformly optimal; Claude Haiku~4.5 prefers $k\!=\!2$. Reporting a per-target best-$k$ would silently reintroduce a fitted parameter and inflate the baseline by up to 6\,pp.}}{14}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ksweep}{{10}{14}{Threshold sweep for the RC$\,\geq \!k$ rule. $k\!=\!1$ is the best fixed default but is not uniformly optimal; Claude Haiku~4.5 prefers $k\!=\!2$. Reporting a per-target best-$k$ would silently reintroduce a fitted parameter and inflate the baseline by up to 6\,pp}{table.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Pipeline accuracy under three extractors. The two non-Anthropic extractors agree closely with each other and both fall below Claude Haiku, on 6 of 7 targets --- a consistent ordering that indicates an extractor-quality gap rather than same-family self-preference. Five self-family controls fell within 0--3\,pp, ruling out a same-family boost.}}{14}{table.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:crossfam}{{11}{14}{Pipeline accuracy under three extractors. The two non-Anthropic extractors agree closely with each other and both fall below Claude Haiku, on 6 of 7 targets --- a consistent ordering that indicates an extractor-quality gap rather than same-family self-preference. Five self-family controls fell within 0--3\,pp, ruling out a same-family boost}{table.11}{}}
